# Capability workspace

This repository is the source for one capforge capability. The server owns its caps directory; you
work in a clone, commit, and push. There is no shared terminal mount.

## The loop

1. `get_contract()` once.
2. `list_capabilities()` for names, active tags and repository URLs.
3. `scaffold_capability(name, description)`; pass `kind="web"` for an HTTP service.
4. `staging(name, action="token")` returns a short-lived token and `git_url`.
5. Clone the repository and work in that clone. Use Basic auth with username
   `basic_username` and the token as password;
   `git --no-pager log` or `GIT_PAGER=cat` avoids the pager hanging a terminal.
6. Edit the uv project; add dependencies with `uv add`, then `uv lock`; commit.
7. `git push origin HEAD:main`.
8. Poll `staging(name, action="latest")`; read `staging(name, action="logs")` on failure.
9. On `promoted`, call `run_capability(name, args)` or `serve_capability(name, action="start")`.

Promotion is automatic on a green pipeline. `install_packages`, `test_capability` and
`promote_capability` are not MCP tools. `capforge promote`/`test` are operator recovery commands.

## Layout

    SKILL.md            name, description, entrypoint, kind, optional data: shared,
                        env: [NAME, ...] for operator-set values
    pyproject.toml      requires-python = ">=3.14,<3.15"; dependencies
    uv.lock             committed; uv sync --frozen is the only install path
    .python-version     3.14
    main.py | app.py    CLI entrypoint / ASGI app
    tests/              pytest; dev group also has pytest-cov
    AGENTS.md           this file

## Process contract (CLI)

- stdin: one JSON object with the call arguments. Never argv or environment variables.
- stdout: one JSON value; the whole output or its last non-empty line.
- stderr: diagnostics, shown to the caller on failure.
- exit 0: success; any other exit is a failure.
- Return `{"error": "..."}` for a handled failure the model should read; raise only for bugs.

## Rules

- Validate every argument; it came from a language model.
- Write runtime files only under `CAPFORGE_DATA_DIR` (per version).
- Add `data: shared` to `SKILL.md` for a stable `CAPFORGE_SHARED_DATA_DIR` across tags. It is
  unversioned; you own concurrency, schema migrations and backup expectations.
- Never write beside `main.py`/`app.py`; the tag is an immutable snapshot and the draft is derived.
- Credentials: declare every variable you read as `env: [NAME, ...]` in `SKILL.md`. The operator
  sets the values in the console; you cannot read or write them and must never commit them.
  Values are shared across tags and injected only into the active tag. Tests and the web boot
  probe get `capforge-test-placeholder`, so never require a real secret at import time.
- Be fast: calls time out (default 120s).
- Be deterministic for `data: version`: same args plus the same data, same result.
- Leave nothing running; a resident service is `kind: web` served with `serve_capability`.
- Landlock confines filesystem access to your code/venv, system libraries and your data dirs;
  network/CPU/memory are not confined.

## Web

`kind: web` requires `uvicorn` in `[project].dependencies` and an ASGI object (`entrypoint: app.py`,
`app: app`). After promotion, `serve_capability(name, action="start")` exposes it at
`/cap/<name>/`. A later promotion does not restart it; use `restart`. Validate every request:
anyone who can reach the proxy is a caller.

## Versioning

A green push is snapshotted into an immutable tag and made active. Older tags are pruned beyond
the keep limit, with their per-version data and unused venvs. `rollback_capability` moves the
pointer without touching the draft or repository. The push pipeline is the only promotion path.
